# Anatomy and Physiology

# Investigations

- **Estimation of pancreatic enzymes in the body** - serum amylase (widely used), serum lipase (more sensitive and specific)
  - **Causes of raised serum amylase:**
    - <u>Pancreatitis</u> - \> 3x ULN highly suspicious, but non-diagnostic
    - <u>Injury to GI tract</u> - mesenteric ischaemia, upper gastrointestinal tract perforation, torsion of intra-abdominal viscera
    - <u>Salivary gland inflammation</u> - any cause of sialododenitis
    - Miscellaneous causes - macroamylasaemia, renal failure, ectopic pregnancy, retroperitoneal haematoma
  - **Diagnosis of pancreatitis** - requires concurrent imaging findings (Atlanta Classification)
- **Pancreatic function test** - assessment of pancreatic exocrine function:
  - Principles - direct stimulation of pancreatic secretion in response to standardised stimulus (physilogical in Lundh test or pharmacological. e.g. CCK, secretin)
  - **Faecal elastase level** - direct measurement of elastase (pancreatic protease) which reflects exocrine function
  - <u>Other indirect measures of pancreatic function</u> - non-specific and highly dependent on intestinal absorptive capacities:
    - **NBT-PABA test** - administration of NBT-PABA which is specifically broken down into PABA, fully absorbed and excreted in urine (urinary levels subsequently measured)
    - **Pancreolauryl test** - works in smilar fasion
- **Ultrasonography** - typically initial Ix ordered to identify dilated biliary systems, presence of gallstones, and presenec or absence of mass in the pancreas
- **Computer tomography** - specific pancreatic protocol to be followed:
  - <u>Non-contrast phase</u> - determine presence of **calcification** in the pancreas and gallbladder
  - <u>Arterial phase and venous phase</u> - absence of contrast enhancement in necrotic tissue in pancreatitis
- **Magnetic resonance cholangiography and pancreatograph** - assessment of anatomy of pancreatic, duct, bile duct etc.
- **Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography** (ERCP) - side-viewing fibreoptic duodenoscope w/ injection of contrast into biliary and pancretic ducts to display its anatomy radiographically
  - <u>Diagnostic radiological features:</u>
    - **Double duct sign** - stricture in both ducts typically seen in MBO
    - **Chronic pancreatitis** - ductal strictures, abnormalities of duct side branches, dilatation of main pancreatic duct due to stone formation etc.
    - **Pancreatic fluid collection and brushing** - for cytological Dx of pancreatic Ca
  - <u>Therapeutic interventions:</u>
    - Stenting - in biliary and pancreatic ducts
- **Endoscopic USG** - special endoscope w/ high-frequency US transducer at tip to assess pancreas and surrounding lymphatics and vasulature through the stomach, allows FNAC and trucut biopsy to be performed under EUS guidance

