# Pancreatitis

- **Definition** - inflammation of the glandular parenchyma of the pancreas, clinically classified as acute pancreatitis and chronic pancreatitis
  - **Acute pancreatitis** - clinically manifests as acute abdomen and is usually a/w raised pancreatic enzyme levels
  - **Chronic pancreatitis** - defined as a continuing chronic inflammatory disease of the pancreas characterised by irreversible morphological change typically resulting in abdominal pain +/- permanent loss of function

## Acute pancreatitis

- **Definition** - acute inflammation of the pancreas
- **Epidemiology** - acutes for 3% of acute abdomen (UK):
  - Incidence - 5-10/100000/y
  - Demographic - occurs at any age, w/ peak in young men and older women
- **Etiology** - two common causes are <u>biliary pancreatitis</u> and <u>alcoholic pancreatitis</u>:
  - **Biliary pancreatitis** (60-70%) - caused by CBD stone (choledocholithiasis)
  - **Alcoholic pancreatitis** (25%) - caused by alcohol abuse
  - Idiopathic - 3rd most common cause (no more than 20% should fall under this category)
  - **Post-ERCP syndrome** - caused by pancreatic ductal disruption leading to enzyme extravastation
  - **Hypercalcaemia** - workup for hyperPTH
  - **Hypertriglyceridaemia**
  - **Drug-induced** - corticosteroids, azathioprine, asparaginase, valproic acid
  - Other rare causes - abdominal trauma, autoimmune pancreatitis, hereditary pancreatitis, pancreatic divisum, malnutrition, drug induced, Ampullary tumour
- **Clinical features of acute pancreatitis:**
  - **Abdominal pain** (typically epigastric pain) - cardial Sx of acute pancreatitis which predominants the clinical presentation:
    - <u>Site</u> - over epigastrium, but may be subsequently shifted to RUQ or felt diffusely through the abdomen
    - <u>Onset</u> - rapid onset to maximal intensity within minutes, acute presentation (persist for h to d)
    - <u>Character</u> - constant, dull pain
    - <u>Radiation</u> - to the back in 50% of case, ocassionally to chest mimicking (MI, pneumonia, pleurisy)
    - <u>Exacerbating and relieving factors</u> - relieved by sitting upwards or leaning forwards
    - <u>Severity</u> - extremely severe
  - **Nausea and repeated vomiting** - typical marked accompaniments, with retching persisting even after being kept NPO and stomach kept emptied by NG aspiration
  - **Hiccups** - due to gastric distension or irritation of diaphragm
- **Signs of acute pancreatitis:**
  - **General appearance** - extremely variable, from one who is well, or one who is gravely ill w/ profuse shock, toxicity and confusion
  - **Vitals** - tachypnoea, tachycardia, +/- hypotension, +/- mild pyrexia (due to inflammation)
  - **Acute swinging fever** - suggestive of acute cholangitis
  - **General examination** - jaundice (suggestive of biliary pancreatitis)
  - **Abdominal examination:**
    - Skin changes (retroperitoneal haemorrhage) - Cullen sign (umbillicus), Grey Turner's sign (flanks)
    - Distension - due to ileus, or rarely ascites (+ve shifting dullness)
    - Palpation - marked guarding typically over epigastrium, whereas rigidity is unusual
  - **Respiratory examination:**
    - Pleural effusion (10-20%) - reduced chest expansion, stony dullness, absent breath sounds +/- mediastinal shift
- **Diagnosis of acute pancreatitis** - <u>Atlanta classification of acute pancreatitis</u> (2012):
  - 2 of 3 of the following criteria:
    - Characteristic abdominal pain
    - Serum pancreatic enzyme (amylase or lipase) \> 3x ULN
    - Characteristic findings of acute pancreatitis on cross-sectional imaging (abdominal US, contrast-enhanced CT abdomen)
- **Ix of acute pancreatitis** - for 1) diagnosis, 2) assessment of severity, and 3) determining etiology:
  - <u>Diagnostic Ix</u> - serum amylase or lipase (\> 3x ULN)
  - <u>Severity Ix</u>:
    - Bloods - CBC, RFT (urea), LDH, CRP, Ca
    - Imaging - contrast CT abdomen (not always indicated), +/- MRCP
  - <u>Etiological Ix</u> - Erect CXR and AXR (r/o ddx of acute abdomen), US liver, TG, Ca, LFT
- **Role of imaging** - for 1) diagnosis, 2) assessment of severity, and 3) identify localised complications:
  - **USG abdomen** (within 24h) - non-specific changes (e.g. swollen pancreas), identify gallstones as potential cause (dilated biliary system)
  - **Contrast-enhanced CT** - for stable patients w/ diagnostic uncertainty, clinically severe acute pancreatitis (on severity scores or progressive clinical deterioration), or suspected local complication
  - +/- MRCP - for suspected biliary pancreatitis

### Management of acute pancreatitis

- **Principles of Mx** - dependent on severity:
  - Mild pancreatitis - conservative Tx
  - Severe pancreatitis - ICU/HDU care
  - Management of underlying cause - e.g. ERCP for biliary pancreatitis
- **Conservative approach:**
  - Keep NPO - principally by 'resting the pancreas'
  - Frequent non-invasive observation - workup by CECT abdomen if clinical deterioration or systemic/ localised complications
  - IV fluid administration - replace the water loss
  - Symptomatic relief:
    - Analgesic - pain relief
    - Anti-emetics - relief N/V
- **Severe acute pancreatitis Mx** - early admission to ICU:
  - Supportive care - O2, aggressive IV fluid administration (against vital signs, CVP), analgesics
  - Routine monitoring - Vital signs, CVP, UO, ABG, clotting, haematocrit, BG, Ca, Mg

### Complications of acute pancreatitis

- **Systemic complications:**
  - Cardiovascular - shock, arrhythmias
  - Pulmonary - ARDS
  - Renal - AKI
  - Haematological - DIC
  - GI - ileus
  - Metabolic - HypoCa (sequestration), Hyperglycaemia, Hyperlipidaemia
  - Neurological - visual disturbance, altered states of consciousness, encephalopathy
- **Local complications** - typically develop after 1st week:
  - <u>Acute fluid collection</u> - acute collection of sterile fluid in or near the pancreas:
    - **Mx** - close monitoring (usually self-resolves), +/- EUS or CT-guided aspiration if cause compressive Sx
    - Cx - abscess (if infected), pseudocyst formation
  - <u>Sterile or infective pancreatic necrosis</u> - focal or diffuse area of non-viable parenchyma characterised by peri-pancreatic fat necrosis:
    - **CT findings** - absence of contrast enhancement on CT (? which phase)
    - **Progression** - sterile to infected (translocation of gut microbiome), <u>mortality rate 50%</u>
    - **Indications of Mx** - showing signs of sepsis:
      - Drainage of infected necrotic pancreas or peripancreatic fluid w/ tube of widest bore - under EUS or CT-guidence (aspiration and sent to microbiology for C/ST)
      - ABx Tx - commenced as per sensitivity testing
      - +/- Pnacreatic necrosectomy - indicated if continued deterioration of sepsis
  - <u>Pancreatic abscess</u> - intra-abdominal collection of pus around the pancreas (infected acute fluid collection of pseudocyst)
    - Mx - as per infected pancreatic necrosis
  - <u>Pancreatic ascites</u> - chronic, generalised peritoneal, enzyme-rich effusion a/w pancreatic duct disruption
    - **Paracentesis** - turbid fluid w/ raised amylase levels
    - **Mx** - ERCP to demonstrate duct disruption and placement of pancreatic stent
  - <u>Pancreatic effusion</u> - pleural effusion occuring w/ or w/o concomitent pancreatic ascites
  - Haemorrhage
  - <u>Portal or splenic vein thrombosis</u> - manifests as pHTN, where Mx is typically conservative (managed as pHTN)
  - <u>Pseudoccyst formation</u> - collection of amylase rich fluid enclosed in wall of fibrous or granulation tissue (typically 4w or more from onset of acute mancreatitis)

